  "narrative": "During the 1930s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were deeply rooted in tradition and identity. The afro, a style created by combing out natural growth of kinky hair, was likely worn by some individuals during this period, although specific documentation from this era is limited. Cornrows, a traditional hairstyle of African braiding practice, continued to be an integral part of many cultures' hair care rituals. Protective hairstyles, designed to minimize manipulation and exposure of Afro-textured hair to environmental elements, were also prevalent. As the natural hair movement was not yet established, chemical relaxers like the Jheri curl, invented later in the 20th century, would have been unknown during this time. Nonetheless, hairstyles such as box braids and locs, which can form naturally or be created with techniques like twisting, backcombing, or crochet, were likely worn by some individuals, reflecting a deep connection to African hair culture and identity.",
